        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Pick up an indoor and an outdoor pickleball side by side and they look almost the same. Hit them and they feel like different sports. Outdoor balls are harder, heavier and pierced with around 40 small holes so they cut through wind; indoor balls are softer, often lighter and have around 26 larger holes so they play slower and more controllably on a gym floor. Neither is better. Each is designed for its setting, and using the wrong one is one of the quickest ways to make a good game feel wrong.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Use outdoor balls outdoors and indoor balls indoors. Outdoor balls typically have around 40 smaller holes and a harder shell, which keeps them flying straight in wind and lets them survive rough court surfaces. Indoor balls typically have around 26 larger holes and a softer feel, which slows the game down and gives more control on smooth gym floors where there is no wind to fight.
          </p>

          <p>
            Both types must meet the same USA Pickleball limits: a weight between 0.78 and 0.935 ounces, a diameter of 2.87 to 2.97 inches, between 26 and 40 holes, and a rebound of 30 to 34 inches from a 78-inch drop. The differences sit inside those limits. That is why both an indoor and an outdoor ball can be tournament legal while playing very differently.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Feature</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Outdoor ball</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Indoor ball</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Holes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 40, smaller</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 26, larger</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Shell</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Harder plastic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Softer plastic</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Weight</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Near the top of the legal range</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often slightly lighter</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pace</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Faster, firmer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Slower, more controllable</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Wind</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Holds its line better</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Pushed around easily</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Wear</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Built for rough surfaces</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wears quickly outdoors</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            If you only play in one place, buy the matching ball and stop there. If you play in both, keep one approved ball of each type. Our guide to the <a href="/best-pickleball-balls/">best pickleball balls</a> picks the strongest options in each category, including a softer outdoor ball for cold weather and a durable ball with a no-crack warranty.
          </p>

          <p>
            The rest of this guide explains what each difference does on court, because understanding why the balls differ makes it easier to choose, and easier to know when bending the rule is fine, such as practising in a sheltered spot with whatever ball you have.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="holes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Hole Count and Size
          </h2>

          <p>
            Holes are the most visible difference, and they do the most work. Air passes through the holes as the ball flies, and the number, size and spacing of those holes decide how much drag the ball has and how stable its flight is. More, smaller holes, as on outdoor balls, reduce drag and help the ball keep its line in moving air.
          </p>

          <p>
            Indoor balls use fewer, larger holes. That increases drag, which slows the ball and gives players more time to react, and it suits indoor play where there is no wind. Many indoor balls, such as the Franklin X-26 and the Gamma Photon Indoor, have 26 holes, the minimum the rules allow, while outdoor models like the Franklin X-40 and Penn 40 use the maximum of 40.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some balls sit in between. The Selkirk Pro S1 has 38 holes of varied sizes, and Selkirk markets it for both indoor and outdoor play. The Dura Fast 40 uses 40 holes in two sizes. Manufacturers use hole patterns to fine-tune flight, so hole count is a guide rather than a rule, but it is the most reliable quick check on a packet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hole quality matters as well as count. Cleanly drilled, evenly spaced holes give a consistent flight, while rough or uneven holes can make a ball wobble. That is one reason approved balls from established brands tend to feel more predictable than cheap unbranded multipacks, even when the hole count is the same.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are unsure which ball you have, count the holes. Around 26 means indoor, around 40 means outdoor. It takes a few seconds and saves a lot of confusion when a bag of mixed balls ends up at the court.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="weight-hardness" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Weight and Hardness
          </h2>

          <p>
            Outdoor balls are usually heavier, around 26 grams near the top of the legal range, and made from harder plastic. The extra weight helps them carry through wind, and the hard shell resists wear from rough acrylic and concrete surfaces. The result is a faster, firmer ball that comes off the paddle with more pace.
          </p>

          <p>
            Indoor balls are often a little lighter and softer. The Gamma Photon Indoor, for example, is listed at about 24 grams, while the Franklin X-26 is listed at about 26 grams, so not every indoor ball is light. What indoor balls share is a softer feel, which makes them easier to control and gentler on the hands and arms during long indoor sessions.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hardness has a downside outdoors. Hard plastics become more brittle in the cold, so outdoor balls are more likely to crack on chilly mornings. Softer outdoor balls, such as the Penn 40, are generally less prone to this, which is why they are popular for winter play. Indoor balls rarely face the same problem, because gyms stay at a stable temperature.
          </p>

          <p>
            Weight and hardness also change how a ball feels on a given paddle. A heavy, hard outdoor ball can make a powerful paddle feel too hot, while a soft indoor ball can make a control paddle feel dead. If you play both settings, it is worth thinking about the ball when you choose a paddle, as our <a href="/best-pickleball-paddle/">pickleball paddle guide</a> discusses.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="wind-pace" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Wind, Pace and Rallies
          </h2>

          <p>
            Wind is the main reason outdoor balls exist. A light ball with large holes is pushed sideways and slowed by a moderate breeze, which turns careful dinks and drops into guesswork. A heavier ball with smaller holes holds its line better, so players can still place the ball with some confidence on a breezy day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pace follows from the same design. Outdoor balls are faster and bounce more firmly, which shortens rallies and rewards aggressive play. Indoor balls are slower and softer, which lengthens rallies and rewards patience and placement. Neither is better; they produce different styles of game, and many players enjoy switching between them.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is why the same group can feel like it is playing two different games indoors and outdoors. Shots that win points outside, like hard drives, are easier to defend inside, while the soft game around the kitchen becomes more important. If you are moving from outdoor to indoor play, expect a few sessions of adjustment.
          </p>

          <p>
            For practice, the wind effect can be useful. Drilling with an outdoor ball on a windy day builds control, and some players keep a heavier practice ball, such as the Onix Pure 2 Outdoor, for exactly that. It is no longer approved for sanctioned play, per Pickleball Central, but it is fine for drills and casual games.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="surfaces" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Court Surfaces
          </h2>

          <p>
            Outdoor courts are usually acrylic-coated asphalt or concrete with a textured finish for grip. That texture is rough on balls, so outdoor balls need hard shells to survive it. An indoor ball used on an outdoor court will scuff and wear quickly, and its softer shell can lose roundness faster on the abrasive surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            Indoor courts are usually wood or synthetic sports flooring, which is smooth. A hard outdoor ball on a smooth floor can skid rather than grip and bounce, which makes it play fast and low. Softer indoor balls grip the floor better and give a more predictable bounce, which is why they suit gyms.
          </p>

          <p>
            Home courts add more variety. A driveway or garage floor is usually smooth concrete, which sits between the two. If you are setting up a court at home, an outdoor ball is usually the better choice on concrete exposed to wind, while an indoor ball can work in a garage. Our guide to <a href="/pickleball-court-dimensions/">pickleball court dimensions</a> covers layout and net height for home setups.
          </p>

          <p>
            Footwear follows the same logic. Indoor and outdoor courts need different soles, and court shoes grip both far better than running shoes. Our guide to the <a href="/best-pickleball-shoes/">best pickleball shoes</a> explains which outsoles suit which surface, because the ball and the shoes both respond to the floor you play on.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="swapping" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Can You Swap Them?
          </h2>

          <p>
            You can, but you will notice. An outdoor ball indoors plays fast and hard and can skid on a smooth floor, which shortens rallies and makes the game feel rushed. It is legal in casual play and fine for a quick session if it is all you have, but most players find it less enjoyable than a proper indoor ball.
          </p>

          <p>
            An indoor ball outdoors is the worse swap. It is pushed around by wind, slowed unpredictably, and worn quickly by rough court surfaces. On a calm day on a smooth surface it can be playable, but on a typical outdoor court it turns the game into a lottery. If you only own one type, an outdoor ball is the more versatile choice.
          </p>

          <p>
            For tournaments, the event chooses the ball, and it will be the right type for the setting. Practise with the type you will play with, ideally the exact model, because switching balls on the day throws off timing. If an event uses a specific approved ball, buy a pack a few weeks ahead.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some balls are marketed for both settings. The Selkirk Pro S1, with 38 holes, is one example. These hybrids are a reasonable compromise for players who switch often and want one bag of balls, but a dedicated ball for each setting will usually feel better to regular players.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">If you own only one ball, make it an outdoor ball.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">An outdoor ball indoors is fast but playable. An indoor ball outdoors is pushed around by wind and worn down by the court surface. Keep one of each if you play both regularly.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="choosing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Choosing for Your Group
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with where you play most. A group that plays mainly on outdoor courts needs an approved outdoor ball, chosen for its pace and how it handles your weather. A group that plays mainly in a gym needs an approved indoor ball, often whatever the gym or league already uses, so everyone practises with the same thing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then think about the problems you have. Outdoor groups that lose balls to cracks in cold weather should look at softer balls or one-piece designs with a warranty. Indoor groups rarely have cracking problems, but they do care about consistency, so sticking with one approved model makes the game more predictable.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, consider the paddles and players. Beginners usually enjoy the slower indoor game or a softer outdoor ball, while competitive players often prefer a fast, firm outdoor ball. If your group is mixed, a middle-of-the-road approved ball keeps everyone happy. New players should also read our <a href="/best-pickleball-paddle-for-beginners/">beginner paddle picks</a> and <a href="/how-to-choose-a-pickleball-paddle/">how to choose a pickleball paddle</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever you choose, buy a small pack first, play with it for a couple of weeks, and only then commit to a bulk pack. Our full guide to the <a href="/best-pickleball-balls/">best pickleball balls</a> lists approved options in each category, with the specs that separate them, so you can match the ball to your court.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Bringing Indoor Balls to an Outdoor Court</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Indoor balls are softer with larger holes, so wind blows them around and rough surfaces wear them down quickly. The game becomes unpredictable and the balls do not last. Keep at least one approved outdoor ball for outdoor courts, and leave indoor balls for the gym.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Hole Count Is the Only Difference</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Hole count is the easiest clue, but weight, hardness and hole size all change how a ball plays. Two 40-hole balls can feel quite different. Read the full specs and, ideally, try a small pack before deciding a ball suits your group.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Practising With a Different Ball Than the Event Uses</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Tournaments choose one approved ball, and switching on the day throws off timing. Find out which ball your event uses and practise with that model for a few weeks beforehand, so pace and bounce feel familiar when it counts.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving Outdoor Balls in the Cold</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Hard outdoor balls become brittle when cold and crack far more easily. A ball left in a car overnight in winter may split in the first game. Store balls at room temperature and warm them in a pocket before playing on chilly mornings.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mixing Ball Types in One Bag</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A bag with indoor and outdoor balls mixed together leads to mismatched games and confusion. Count the holes and separate them, or use different colours for each type, so you always grab the right ball for the court you are on.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use outdoor pickleballs indoors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, but they play fast and hard on smooth gym floors and can skid instead of gripping. It is fine for casual play if it is all you have. Most players prefer a dedicated indoor ball for regular indoor sessions because it gives longer rallies and more control.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use indoor pickleballs outdoors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can on a calm day, but it is the worse swap. Indoor balls are pushed around by wind and worn quickly by rough outdoor surfaces. If you only own one type of ball, an outdoor ball is the more versatile choice for most players.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many holes does an indoor pickleball have?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Indoor pickleballs typically have around 26 larger holes, which is the minimum USA Pickleball allows. The Franklin X-26 and Gamma Photon Indoor both have 26. Outdoor balls typically have around 40 smaller holes, the maximum allowed.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why do outdoor pickleballs have more holes?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                More, smaller holes reduce drag and help the ball hold a stable line in moving air. Combined with a heavier, harder shell, that lets outdoor balls cope with wind and rough surfaces. Indoor balls use fewer, larger holes because they do not need to fight wind.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are indoor pickleballs softer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Generally, yes. Indoor balls use softer plastic for a slower, more controllable game and better grip on smooth floors. Outdoor balls use harder plastic to resist wear from textured courts, which also makes them faster and more prone to cracking in cold weather.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do indoor and outdoor balls weigh the same?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both must weigh between 0.78 and 0.935 ounces. Outdoor balls usually sit near the top of that range, around 26 grams, while indoor balls are often slightly lighter. Some indoor balls, like the Franklin X-26, are listed at around 26 grams too, so check the specific ball.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which ball should beginners use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Beginners usually find indoor balls or softer outdoor balls easier, because the slower pace gives more time to react. Use the type that matches your court, and choose a softer, approved ball for outdoor play if your group finds the hardest balls too fast.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

