          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Indoor and outdoor pickleball balls are built for different conditions. Outdoor balls are harder, heavier and use around 40 small holes to handle wind and rough courts; indoor balls are softer with around 26 larger holes for a slower game on smooth floors.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Use the matching ball for your court, keep one of each if you play both, and if you can only own one, make it an outdoor ball. Practise with the exact ball your events use, and store outdoor balls at room temperature to avoid cold-weather cracks.
          </p>
